00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:57.040
Toby has a fantastic talking head during all this chaos.

501
00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:01.740
And he's like, you know, the simple solution would just be to open a window, but none of our

502
00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:02.500
windows open.

503
00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:04.840
That's a little creepy.

504
00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:05.980
It really is.

505
00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:10.900
Well, Angela, then you have a talking head where you say, I can't even say it.

506
00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:13.080
This is one of my most quoted lines to me.

507
00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:17.740
You know, like when you meet fans of the show and they quote a line to you, this is one of

508
00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:18.160
the lines.

509
00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:21.680
Strangers would come up to me and be like, poop is raining from the ceilings.

510
00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:22.480
Poop.

511
00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:23.580
It's so good.

512
00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:27.120
Angela, we had a fan question from Jackie Kay.

513
00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:32.180
How many takes did it require for Angela to get the poop is raining from the ceiling talking

514
00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:32.500
head?

515
00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:39.800
We did a few because we were playing around with what type of distress was this for Angela?

516
00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:41.820
Was she angry or terrified?

517
00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:43.720
Scared, scared, annoyed.

518
00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:45.120
So we did a few.

519
00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:48.300
And the one they picked was where I'm actually concerned and scared.

520
00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:51.260
And I have my rain bonnet on.

521
00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:55.240
I was going to say the rain bonnet is everything.

522
00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:56.080
Everything.

523
00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.780
Angela, my grandmother had one of those.

524
00:24:59.000 --> 00:24:59.320
Yes.

525
00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:02.160
My grandmother got her hair done once a week.

526
00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:03.360
Same.

527
00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.120
And it would be the same hairdo.

528
00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:06.740
It was set for the whole week.

529
00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:08.100
Yeah.

530
00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:09.780
That's what the ladies used to do.

531
00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:11.660
They used to have their weekly appointment.

532
00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:16.060
They'd get their hair washed and set and then just teased and sprayed.

533
00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:16.480
Yeah.

534
00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:21.580
And then my grandma had this little rain bonnet folded up in her purse.

535
00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:25.040
And if there was even a sprinkle, she would throw that thing on.

536
00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:25.460
Yes.

537
00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:26.560
Same with my grandmother.

538
00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:31.540
I have so many memories of her putting it on, going to church because there was just maybe

539
00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:33.880
a tiny, tiny bit of miSt.

540
00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:37.000
I was just tickled by this scene because it made me think of my grandmother.

541
00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.820
And I love that Angela Martin keeps one on hand.

542
00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.080
She has a rain bonnet ready to go.

543
00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:45.900
When I saw it, I thought we need to bring back this rain bonnet.

544
00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:46.920
I looked online.

545
00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:48.500
There's a lot you can buy now.

546
00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:50.640
And you know I love a good pun.

547
00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:55.900
There is a company that makes one and they call it the hairbrella.

548
00:25:56.060 --> 00:25:57.040
I want a hairbrella.

549
00:25:57.040 --> 00:26:03.220
Well, Jim calls animal control and they say they're going to be there by six o'clock.

550
00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:05.300
But Dwight finds this unacceptable.

551
00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:05.740
Yeah.

552
00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.440
But this is when Jim starts his prank on Dwight.

553
00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:16.480
He's distracted by this bite on his neck and he's just feeling like really tingly and

554
00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:17.920
strangely powerful.

555
00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:19.340
Huh.

556
00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:20.540
Can't explain it.

557
00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.060
That's all he has to do.

558
00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:23.920
And Dwight fills in all the blanks.

559
00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:30.220
Well, now back at the business school, Michael is in like a sound booth watching Ryan sort

560
00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:32.540
of give an introduction is what he thinks, right?

561
00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:37.720
But Ryan is basically telling the classroom that Dunder Mifflin can't compete with the

562
00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:38.480
bigger chains.

563
00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:42.040
He thinks this whole business is going to be obsolete in like five years.

564
00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:46.800
And in fact, he thinks the management isn't flexible and able to adapt.

565
00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:47.980
And you know what I wrote?

566
00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:49.220
What?

567
00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:51.440
Ryan, how dare you?

568
00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:53.780
You know what this means to Michael.

569
00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:56.260
How dare you set him up like that?

570
00:26:56.720 --> 00:27:00.920
Look, if that conversation was going to come out in the classroom on its own, fine.

571
00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:03.380
But Ryan could have set him up differently.

572
00:27:03.740 --> 00:27:05.420
He could have said, you know what, guys?

573
00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:07.960
Dunder Mifflin is a small company.

574
00:27:08.240 --> 00:27:09.880
It has a harder time competing.

575
00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:10.860
But you know what?

576
00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:12.500
Management really believes in it.

577
00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:15.680
They really believe in like a one-on-one personal connection.

578
00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:21.460
And my boss is someone is he's sort of part of a dying breed of a traditional salesman.

579
00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:23.240
He's being a total punk in this moment.

580
00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:23.800
I think so.

581
00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:29.580
Well, then when Michael takes the stage, thinking he's been introduced in a totally different,

582
00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:36.360
more flattering way, he enters with his own musical fanfare.

583
00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:37.540
He shows up.

584
00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:38.060
That he has brought with him.

585
00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:39.740
He shows up with his boombox.

586
00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:40.820
Yeah.

587
00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:42.400
He's playing like classical music.

588
00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:44.520
That's a bold move.

589
00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:50.620
No matter how Ryan set you up, even if Ryan had painted you in an amazing light, to walk

590
00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:56.980
in with your boombox playing your intro song is really, it's a really bold move.

591
00:27:56.980 --> 00:28:02.520
Knowing everything we know about Michael, it's surprising that he didn't show up dressed as

592
00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:04.900
a character to inspire the students.

593
00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:06.800
But he has come as himself.

594
00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:08.720
But he does bring props.

595
00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:10.280
Oh, does he ever.

596
00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:16.360
Well, he starts by tearing up someone's book as a way of inspiring them.

597
00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:22.960
And then he moves into his candy bar runner pun.

598
00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:27.980
Then he lets the classroom know there are four kinds of businesses.

599
00:28:28.280 --> 00:28:29.800
Number one, tourism.

600
00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:31.900
Number two, food service.

601
00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:33.920
Number three, railroads.

602
00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:35.780
Number four, sales.

603
00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:40.040
Number five, hospital and manufacturing.

604
00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:41.620
And then also air travel.

605
00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:45.300
My favorite is hospital slash manufacturing.

606
00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:46.200
I know.

607
00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:52.160
So then he moves into his candy bar puns.

608
00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:54.440
Oh, yeah.

609
00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:55.520
So good.

610
00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:56.240
So good.

611
00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:57.780
Do you know who wrote this whole scene?

612
00:28:58.480 --> 00:28:59.060
Who?

613
00:28:59.300 --> 00:28:59.940
Jen Salata.

614
00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:02.900
Oh, that makes so much sense.

615
00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:04.320
It's so great, isn't it?

616
00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:06.420
Well, we had a lot of people write in.

617
00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:14.120
Sammy Sauls, Allie Stecklin, Lily McGrath all asked how much of Michael's speech to Ryan's

618
00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:15.880
business class was improvised.

619
00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:18.580
Guys, basically none of it.

620
00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:21.300
It was almost like I went to the script.

621
00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:23.080
It's basically all scripted.

622
00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:27.060
What you see there in the episode, scripted.

623
00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:27.620
Yeah.

624
00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:31.160
You know, we didn't get to be there, Jenna, to be in that classroom.

625
00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:32.780
It looked like so much fun.

626
00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:37.320
So we asked Brent Forrester, what was it like to shoot those scenes with Michael and the

627
00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:37.700
students?

628
00:29:37.900 --> 00:29:38.860
And here's what he had to say.

629
00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:45.720
Filming the business school scenes with Steve Carell in that lecture hall full of kids

630
00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:15.700
was really unforgettable.

631
00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:19.600
would have to pretend that they were very unimpressed by his lecture.

632
00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:21.220
And so they would sit there in silence.

633
00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:23.760
And then the assistant director would call cut.

634
00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:29.040
And then the whole group of kids would just burst out into laughter that they'd been repressing

635
00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:30.360
through the whole scene.

636
00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:35.520
So over and over again, it was Steve performs, silence, cut, huge laugh.

637
00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:41.920
Then in the final take of the final scene, Steve performs, there's silence.

638
00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:43.120
The AD says, cut.

639
00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:44.280
That's a wrap for Steve.

640
00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:47.060
The audience of kids burst into laughter.

641
00:30:47.380 --> 00:30:51.200
They stand up and they give him this ovation that went on forever.

642
00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:56.080
I was standing next to Steve at the time and I saw the tears come to his eyes.

643
00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:57.300
It was so beautiful.

644
00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:00.740
One of the highlights for sure of my time on The Office.

645
00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:02.220
Oh my gosh.

646
00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:03.240
I wish I could have been there.

647
00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.800
I bet it felt so magical to those kids, you know, and to Steve too.

648
00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:10.680
He basically had this whole audience just for himself.

649
00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:16.700
And kind of what Brent was talking about, which is that Steve started in live performance.

650
00:31:17.780 --> 00:31:20.260
And I did too with theater, Angela.

651
00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:25.780
And it was a big adjustment to be sort of acting into a void, which is the camera.

652
00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:28.080
You don't get to see your jokes land.

653
00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:33.820
You don't get to hear people in their living rooms at home, you know, moving through your

654
00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:36.500
performance with you like you do in a theater situation.

655
00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:39.640
So it's always fun to get to perform for a live audience.

656
00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:40.620
Always, always.

657
00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:44.820
That was my whole, you know, every week doing an improv show.

658
00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:50.740
And it is a real energy to have a room like that with you and in the moment with you.

659
00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:53.220
It just always kind of makes my skin tingle.

660
00:31:53.540 --> 00:31:53.860
All right.

661
00:31:53.980 --> 00:31:56.300
So should we go check in at Dunder Mifflin now?

662
00:31:56.520 --> 00:32:03.480
Because Dwight is making a trap for the bat using a box and some glue.

663
00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:03.880
Yeah.

664
00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:04.900
So terrible.

665
00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:05.240
Poor bat.

666
00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:06.080
Yeah.

667
00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:12.700
And in the background, Jim and Karen are continuing this prank.

668
00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:13.460
Yeah.

669
00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:15.280
Karen is doing it, not Pam.

670
00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:17.520
I noticed.

671
00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:20.820
And I don't, I mean, I don't want to be.

672
00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.020
Oh, here it comes.

673
00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:23.400
I mean.

674
00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:26.220
Was Pam better at pranking?

675
00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.020
You said it.

676
00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:39.780
I mean, yes, I feel like Jim and Karen's prank is a little presentational and a little on the nose.

677
00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:49.400
And I think that when Pam pranks Dwight with Jim, it's more subtle and creative.

678
00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:50.120
I don't know.

679
00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:50.440
Yeah.

680
00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:59.120
Well, you know, it's really hard to have the finesse of like drinking your water by the water cooler and, you know,

681
00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:04.080
covering up whatever Jim is doing by drinking a little cup of water and looking around.

682
00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:05.320
I mean, that is hard.

683
00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:07.440
That doesn't come natural.

684
00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:08.060
I knew you were going to bring that up.

685
00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:13.820
I knew you were going to bring that up because it's the one time that Pam is terrible at pranking.

686
00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:15.900
It makes me laugh.

687
00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:16.880
It makes me laugh.

688
00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:18.780
But I'm sorry.

689
00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:21.500
Her other pranks are very good.

690
00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:28.400
But yes, so they have this whole bit where like Jim can't pick up her piece of bread.

691
00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:29.320
It's white hot.

692
00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:30.580
Because it's garlic bread.

693
00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:32.680
And it's burning his hands.

694
00:33:32.820 --> 00:33:34.660
And she's like, this is ice cold.

695
00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:42.540
And then we go into, I mean, it's a little bit of heartbreak in the break room again.

696
00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:43.200
With Toby?

697
00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:44.000
But it's Toby.

698
00:33:44.440 --> 00:33:44.760
Oh, yeah.

699
00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:46.340
It's Toby has some heartbreak.

700
00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:49.460
I mean, you guys, Toby is in love with Pam.

701
00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:58.420
And here is his opportunity to see her outside of work and maybe dazzle her somehow with his knowledge of art and support her.

702
00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:02.040
And it's the same evening of his daughter's play.

703
00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:03.280
He's devastated.

704
00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:07.920
And he's even trying to think of a way to get out of going to see his child's play.

705
00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:09.340
I know.

706
00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:12.240
He's like, it's just we need to support local art.

707
00:34:12.240 --> 00:34:16.300
And what they're doing is not art.

708
00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:17.740
Let's be honeSt.

709
00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:22.540
And Pam's like, you should go see your child's play.

710
00:34:23.240 --> 00:34:24.040
Yes.

711
00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:25.860
Yes.

712
00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:27.100
Oh.

713
00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:27.940
Oh.

714
00:34:28.220 --> 00:34:35.240
Well, lady, while this is going on, back at the school, they're starting to get into the Q&A part, right?

715
00:34:35.240 --> 00:34:37.960
And the students are starting to ask Michael questions.

716
00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:39.440
Things are kind of falling apart.

717
00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:42.120
There's a moment, Jenna, I love so much.

718
00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:47.140
I love it when Michael says, real business is done on paper.

719
00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:48.380
Write that down.

720
00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:49.840
And they all type.

721
00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:51.440
Yeah.

722
00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:52.920
It's just the sound of keyboards.

723
00:34:54.240 --> 00:34:54.660
Yes.

724
00:34:54.660 --> 00:34:59.000
That's a very fun joke in the script, too.

725
00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:02.760
Like, it says, the students all get out their computers and begin typing.

726
00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:05.480
It's so good.

727
00:35:05.760 --> 00:35:12.920
So the students start asking him, how would you keep a client from leaving Dunder Mifflin for a nationwide chain?

728
00:35:13.480 --> 00:35:17.760
And Michael's like, I don't need to persuade them to come back.

729
00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:23.220
They'll just come back eventually when they realize what a debacle it is to go to anyone else.

730
00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:26.260
And then the student's like, well, has anyone ever come back?

731
00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:29.940
And Michael's like, I don't want them back because they're stupid.

732
00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:31.760
Michael is starting to take this personal.

733
00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:33.840
He's getting his feelings hurt.

734
00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:42.800
And that is when one of the students points out, even your own employee says that Dunder Mifflin is going to be obsolete in five to ten years.

735
00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:44.160
Oh.

736
00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:47.840
I mean, this is just like a sucker punch.

737
00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:50.240
Michael is so hurt.

738
00:35:50.600 --> 00:35:50.760
Yeah.

739
00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:50.810
Yeah.

740
00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:56.740
So now, back at the office, Dwight has formed an allegiance with Creed.

741
00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:00.440
What I love about this, Jenna, is that Dwight couldn't even get a sentence out.

742
00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:02.660
He was like, listen, Creed, I need an alliance.

743
00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:03.200
He's like, sure.

744
00:36:04.580 --> 00:36:07.320
Like, Creed is just like, let's to go.

745
00:36:07.680 --> 00:36:09.100
He is up for it.

746
00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:10.720
He has a drawer of tools.

747
00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:12.000
So does Dwight.

748
00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:13.880
I mean, they're meant to be.

749
00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:17.920
Dwight asks him, can you turn a wooden mop handle into a stake?

750
00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:19.960
And Creed is like, what size?

751
00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:20.320
Yeah.

752
00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:24.060
He's got a drawer of mung beans and weaponry, apparently.

753
00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:32.000
Well, Jenna, I think this is a great time to take a break because when we come back, Dwight and Creed are going to catch a bat.

754
00:36:41.870 --> 00:36:42.750
All right, guys.

755
00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:44.910
So Creed and Dwight are on a mission.

756
00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:50.530
They are going to catch this bat before 6 p.m., before the professionals of animal control arrive.

757
00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:53.030
They're going to take care of this problem.

758
00:36:53.030 --> 00:36:57.030
And Kelly begs them because Creed has now gone into the supply closet.

759
00:36:57.210 --> 00:36:58.490
He's grabbing some kind of spray.

760
00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:00.770
And Kelly's like, do not hurt that bat.

761
00:37:00.970 --> 00:37:02.610
It has feelings.

762
00:37:02.770 --> 00:37:06.830
And she just makes this big, like, plea for the bat's safety.

763
00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:10.530
And it comes flying into the main area.

764
00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:14.330
And then Kelly immediately screams, kill it, kill it, kill it.

765
00:37:14.330 --> 00:37:17.230
Yes, exactly.

766
00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:24.730
The bat flies into the little kitchen area and Kevin closes the door.

767
00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:31.350
So now that bat is trapped inside and Kevin is like, oh, my gosh, I am a hero.

768
00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:32.830
I am a hero.

769
00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:37.910
Kevin's day has gone from a near-death experience to being a hero.

770
00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:39.350
He is having a day.

771
00:37:40.010 --> 00:37:43.030
Why do I feel like that's, like, Kevin's every day?

772
00:37:43.210 --> 00:37:49.650
Every day Kevin thinks he's a hero over a really mundane thing that's not quite heroic.

773
00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:51.670
You might be right.

774
00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:55.070
Yeah, I mean, Kevin thinks he saved everyone in the office.

775
00:37:55.350 --> 00:37:59.010
But, guys, not everyone was out of the kitchen.

776
00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:01.250
Meredith had gone to the bathroom.

777
00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:06.870
Well, we're going to leave her in there for a while because we're going back to Ryan's class.

778
00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:08.290
It's getting ugly.

779
00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:09.110
It's getting ugly.

780
00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:10.730
Michael is grasping at straws.

781
00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:11.690
He's so offended.

782
00:38:11.690 --> 00:38:12.910
He's taking it personal.

783
00:38:13.330 --> 00:38:15.510
And he's just started saying all kinds of stuff.

784
00:38:15.910 --> 00:38:20.610
Yeah, he's like, guys, this is a David and Goliath situation.

785
00:38:20.610 --> 00:38:23.210
But remember, David always wins.

786
00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:25.250
And it's just like America.

787
00:38:25.550 --> 00:38:28.230
America is facing five Goliaths.

788
00:38:28.370 --> 00:38:28.790
Five.

789
00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:30.450
And then he lists them off.

790
00:38:30.610 --> 00:38:35.190
It's like Al-Qaeda, global warming, sex predators, mercury poisoning.

791
00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:39.930
This is when Michael points out, you know what?

792
00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:42.710
Ryan has never made a sale.

793
00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:46.850
And he started a fire trying to make a cheesy pita.

794
00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:48.410
That made me laugh out loud.

795
00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:50.810
And then he said, and everyone thinks he's a tease.

796
00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:51.670
Amazing.

797
00:38:52.030 --> 00:38:54.410
And then he says, you know what?

798
00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:55.890
Ryan doesn't know anything.

799
00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:57.210
And neither do you.

800
00:38:57.430 --> 00:38:58.570
So suck on that.

801
00:38:58.730 --> 00:39:00.330
And he storms out of the classroom.

802
00:39:00.330 --> 00:39:01.470
He is so hurt.

803
00:39:01.670 --> 00:39:03.470
If you're Ryan, what are you thinking right now?

804
00:39:03.530 --> 00:39:07.470
Because you now know you rode there with him.

805
00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:11.150
You are going to have to ride back to work with this person.

806
00:39:11.150 --> 00:39:12.910
That is uncomfortable.

807
00:39:13.190 --> 00:39:16.330
Michael tells Ryan, business is always personal, Ryan.

808
00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:18.570
I mean, that says everything you need to know about Michael.

809
00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:19.610
But we know this.

810
00:39:19.930 --> 00:39:21.650
Well, I respect that about him.

811
00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:23.910
I respect that about Michael.

812
00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:25.910
That business isn't business.

813
00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:27.430
Business is always personal.

814
00:39:27.850 --> 00:39:30.050
And that, you know, he's going to say this later.

815
00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:31.990
A business is made up of people.

816
00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:33.130
I liked it.

817
00:39:33.270 --> 00:39:36.770
He tells Ryan, when we get back to the office, you better pack your things.

818
00:39:36.770 --> 00:39:40.110
This now starts one of my favorite storylines.

819
00:39:40.670 --> 00:39:43.090
Pam is finally at her art show.

820
00:39:43.470 --> 00:39:45.430
She's waiting for people to arrive.

821
00:39:45.930 --> 00:39:47.710
And we had a fan question.

822
00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:54.730
Alex Lane, Ryan Moberly, and Shelley Jones would like to know what kind of discussions occurred

823
00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:56.950
regarding Pam's art show outfit.

824
00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:00.330
And here, this little like swoopy side ponytail.

825
00:40:00.910 --> 00:40:05.370
Pam just is trying to look like she lives, I feel like, in the East Village of New York.

826
00:40:05.370 --> 00:40:11.310
And yes, well, they wanted to know if I had any input into what artsy Pam would look like

827
00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:11.870
that night.

828
00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:13.750
Oh, yes, I did.

829
00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:15.130
And it was so much fun.

830
00:40:15.730 --> 00:40:18.650
Carrie Bennett and I, we had the best time.

831
00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:25.230
She came up with this idea of kind of like this, this beatnik turtleneck.

832
00:40:25.450 --> 00:40:25.750
Yeah.

833
00:40:26.070 --> 00:40:29.090
With the little jumper over top.

834
00:40:29.090 --> 00:40:36.230
And then Kim Ferry and I thought, what is the hairstyle that coordinates with this little

835
00:40:36.230 --> 00:40:37.210
hipster outfit?

836
00:40:37.210 --> 00:40:39.250
And we thought low side pony.

837
00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:41.190
And it, I think it works.

838
00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:43.490
It's not just any low side pony, though.

839
00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:44.710
It's got a little bit of curl.

840
00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:47.070
It's kind of relaxed.

841
00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:47.470
Yeah.

842
00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:47.810
Yeah.

843
00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:48.930
It's smoothed out.

844
00:40:49.090 --> 00:40:52.970
It's not her like frizzy curly hair that we normally see her with.

845
00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:56.850
She is ready to walk out of that art show into 1960 something.

846
00:40:57.290 --> 00:40:58.370
It's so sweet.

847
00:40:58.370 --> 00:40:59.890
I absolutely loved it.

848
00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:03.230
And you can see all of Pam's art behind her.

849
00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:12.010
It's a series of watercolors of, you know, simple, ordinary items and things, which I think

850
00:41:12.010 --> 00:41:18.030
kind of goes to that whole theme of the show and Pam finding beauty in ordinary things.

851
00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:23.510
And I liked the juxtaposition that, you know, Pam's wall is like a coffee mug and a stapler

852
00:41:23.510 --> 00:41:28.590
and a vase holding flowers and, you know, a painting of Dunder Mifflin.

853
00:41:28.590 --> 00:41:35.630
And then just to the left, whoever's art display that was is like aliens or like an alien robot.

854
00:41:35.690 --> 00:41:37.290
I'm not really sure what it is.

855
00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:43.330
Well, we had a fan question from Molly Warner, who says, according to Dunderpedia,

856
00:41:43.610 --> 00:41:51.810
Joss Whedon was not satisfied with the original art that was produced for Pam at the art show and that new art had to be made.

857
00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:57.690
Can you clarify what that original art was and why it had to be changed?

858
00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:03.210
Well, we asked Brent about this and here's what he told us.

859
00:42:03.210 --> 00:42:15.790
What I remember most about Joss Whedon's directing of this episode was a crisis that happened around one of the watercolor drawings that appears in Pam's art show.

860
00:42:16.030 --> 00:42:24.090
Now, these watercolor drawings were created by the art department at the office and they appear in the background of all of the shots of Jenna.

861
00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:28.890
And, you know, as background items, they might not seem that important, but when we got on set,

862
00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:36.470
there was one drawing that Joss just strongly felt that Pam's character would not have done.

863
00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:43.290
It was a watercolor drawing of a church and he just felt it was out of character for her.

864
00:42:43.510 --> 00:42:48.170
And you have to imagine when you're shooting a television episode, the time pressure is so intense.

865
00:42:48.170 --> 00:42:51.610
You do not have time to stop and mess around.

866
00:42:51.610 --> 00:42:59.670
And Joss, however, felt so passionately about this picture that he stopped production and said, I need a new picture.

867
00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.590
I would like a picture of a stapler.

868
00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:04.650
That's what I think that Pam would have drawn.

869
00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:07.250
And like I say, the whole production shut down.

870
00:43:07.330 --> 00:43:09.590
They brought the executive producer, Greg Daniels, to set.

871
00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:13.090
They had a big discussion about it, but Joss would not back down.

872
00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:14.630
And we were on location.

873
00:43:14.630 --> 00:43:16.190
We didn't have the art department with us.

874
00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:18.170
Who is going to draw a picture of a stapler?

875
00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:22.270
So the assistant director, we were on a location at a university.

876
00:43:22.450 --> 00:43:29.310
The assistant director went into a classroom full of art students and said, is anyone here able to draw a watercolor of a stapler?

877
00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:33.110
And they pulled some young woman out and they paid her 50 bucks or something.

878
00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:37.550
And she just cranked out this picture of a stapler that you see in the background of all of Pam's shots.

879
00:43:37.550 --> 00:43:42.310
And afterwards, I was riding back in the van to home base with Joss.

880
00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:44.170
And I said, Joss, what was all that about?

881
00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:46.710
Why did you get so intense about this?

882
00:43:46.870 --> 00:43:56.150
And he said, Brent, in my experience as a director, every single time that I have felt strongly about some detail of the shoot,

883
00:43:56.150 --> 00:44:06.030
and I have allowed myself to be persuaded out of it because of time pressure, 100% of the time, I have intensely regretted making that choice.

884
00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:10.670
And so now I understand that I should never compromise when I feel something strongly as a director.

885
00:44:10.990 --> 00:44:12.450
It's a great lesson.

886
00:44:12.690 --> 00:44:15.430
In my limited experience as a director, I've never forgotten it.

887
00:44:15.530 --> 00:44:17.870
I think it's a really good lesson for all of us as artists.

888
00:44:18.170 --> 00:44:19.390
Stick to your guns.

889
00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:22.050
If you feel it passionately, you're probably right.

890
00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:31.610
But I have to say, Angela, the watercolor that stood out to me the most when we first flash up to her wall was that stapler.

891
00:44:31.910 --> 00:44:32.890
Yeah, me too.

892
00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:35.270
It was absolutely what Pam would have done.

893
00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:39.870
Because Pam, we already established, she's sort of drawing things in her world.

894
00:44:40.030 --> 00:44:42.950
And she sketched a tape dispenser.

895
00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:44.550
It would make sense to me.

896
00:44:44.730 --> 00:44:48.830
And then, you know, Jim, I feel like that stapler also represents Jim.

897
00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:53.910
Jim put that stapler in Dwight's Jell-O mold, you know?

898
00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:56.930
I never thought of that.

899
00:44:57.070 --> 00:44:58.110
Yeah, that's what I thought.

900
00:44:58.230 --> 00:45:02.090
I thought Pam painted that stapler because she was thinking of Jim.

901
00:45:03.810 --> 00:45:05.190
I just got chills.

902
00:45:05.530 --> 00:45:09.890
Well, I got a little lowdown on the Dunder Mifflin painting from Kentopedia.

903
00:45:09.890 --> 00:45:21.650
He said that that is not actually a watercolor, but it was a photograph of our building that they manipulated to look like a watercolor.

904
00:45:22.410 --> 00:45:26.430
Oh, like just some fancy kind of graphics.

905
00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:28.010
Yeah.

906
00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:35.570
So no one ever did a from blank page watercolor of the building.

907
00:45:35.570 --> 00:45:41.790
You know, that time, a while back, Jenna invited me to do a wine and canvas night.

908
00:45:42.850 --> 00:45:43.370
Yes.

909
00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:45.070
And it was so fun.

910
00:45:45.170 --> 00:45:49.050
It was like part of a mall, but you entered from outside, right?

911
00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:49.790
Yeah.

912
00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:52.270
It was a mall in Pasadena.

913
00:45:52.810 --> 00:45:53.110
Yeah.

914
00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:57.530
And we went and you kind of just sort of pick a random night to go.

915
00:45:57.710 --> 00:45:59.690
And I wasn't really sure what we were painting.

916
00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:03.990
And then we found out we were painting cats on a fence at nighttime.

917
00:46:04.930 --> 00:46:05.450
Yes.

918
00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:06.970
And I still have mine.

919
00:46:07.170 --> 00:46:08.830
My husband loves it so much.

920
00:46:08.950 --> 00:46:11.250
He hung it by where he feeds the cats.

921
00:46:11.250 --> 00:46:15.270
So the cats see the cats on the fence as they eat their dinner.

922
00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:16.090
It's kind of funny.

923
00:46:16.450 --> 00:46:21.050
But I became sort of really interested in this wine and canvas night.

924
00:46:21.190 --> 00:46:23.950
We were talking about doing another one, maybe inviting a few friends.

925
00:46:24.110 --> 00:46:30.690
I found that they do wine and canvas and everyone can paint the Dunder Mifflin building.

926
00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:32.210
Oh, my gosh.

927
00:46:32.350 --> 00:46:33.370
That is amazing.

928
00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:34.810
Wouldn't you want to do that?

929
00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:36.290
I would love to do that.

930
00:46:36.530 --> 00:46:42.210
Angela, I think it would be really weird if you and I showed up at a wine and canvas and

931
00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:43.610
painted Dunder Mifflin.

932
00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:45.710
There's like a room of people.

933
00:46:45.890 --> 00:46:46.770
Like you sign up.

934
00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:47.310
We showed up.

935
00:46:47.410 --> 00:46:48.730
There were probably 30 people there.

936
00:46:48.970 --> 00:46:51.030
And we each had a little seating assignment.

937
00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:52.550
We had our canvas and our paints.

938
00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:54.590
And then they pass around wine.

939
00:46:55.050 --> 00:46:56.670
And the teacher is at the front.

940
00:46:56.790 --> 00:46:59.010
She had a headset on, remember, so we could all hear.

941
00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:03.210
So you and I would be in a room with like 30 other people, total strangers.

942
00:47:03.550 --> 00:47:08.250
I totally remember it because my daughter has always, from the time she was born, loved

943
00:47:08.250 --> 00:47:08.650
cats.

944
00:47:08.830 --> 00:47:09.970
She loves cats.

945
00:47:09.970 --> 00:47:12.050
And I knew she was going to love this painting.

946
00:47:12.110 --> 00:47:16.730
And I didn't want my son to feel left out when I came home from my painting night.

947
00:47:16.730 --> 00:47:26.290
So on my canvas, I added a little spaceship out in the sky because he loved outer space.

948
00:47:26.450 --> 00:47:29.590
So I was like, OK, well, this will bring him into this painting.

949
00:47:29.750 --> 00:47:32.010
And we have it in our playroom.

950
00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:33.030
I love it.

951
00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:33.490
Hanging up.

952
00:47:33.670 --> 00:47:34.250
But I do.

953
00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:36.010
I want to do a wine and canvas night.

954
00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:37.670
And I want to paint Dunder Mifflin, Jenna.

955
00:47:38.010 --> 00:47:40.210
I can't say I want to do that.

956
00:47:41.510 --> 00:47:43.410
You don't want to paint Dunder Mifflin?

957
00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:44.670
I know.

958
00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:46.610
Well, you have it, don't you?

959
00:47:46.610 --> 00:47:47.550
You own it.

960
00:47:47.670 --> 00:47:48.070
I do.

961
00:47:48.350 --> 00:47:49.570
I already have a painting.

962
00:47:49.570 --> 00:47:50.190
You own it.

963
00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:51.670
I was going to leave that for the end.

964
00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:52.670
You own it.

965
00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:55.970
But also, Jenna, what if your painting was horrible?

966
00:47:59.170 --> 00:48:01.810
I love your attempt at painting Dunder Mifflin.

967
00:48:01.950 --> 00:48:02.530
No, I'm kidding.

968
00:48:02.830 --> 00:48:05.310
Well, I will say this, too.

969
00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:09.330
In this pandemic, my daughter's gotten really into watercolor painting.

970
00:48:09.630 --> 00:48:16.490
And so it's very funny to me that I, as Jenna, am now sitting around every day doing

971
00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:19.030
watercolor paintings like my character Pam did.

972
00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:22.150
And I think I've gotten really good at doing flowers.

973
00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:24.390
I've actually watched some tutorial videos.

974
00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:29.710
Well, maybe I'll watch a tutorial video on painting Dunder Mifflin and I'll...

975
00:48:29.710 --> 00:48:31.090
There must be one.

976
00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:32.910
I'll paint it and show it to you.

977
00:48:33.670 --> 00:48:34.590
There you go.

978
00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:35.070
Okay.

979
00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:35.610
Sorry.

980
00:48:35.790 --> 00:48:36.790
Where are we, lady?

981
00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:43.370
Well, back at Dunder Mifflin, Meredith is still hiding from the bat and everyone is just

982
00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:44.570
leaving for the day.

983
00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:46.410
Everyone's like, bye.

984
00:48:46.950 --> 00:48:48.570
Creed is like, later, Mary Beth.

985
00:48:49.970 --> 00:48:50.410
Yes.

986
00:48:50.590 --> 00:48:52.390
People wanted to know if that was improvisation.

987
00:48:52.470 --> 00:48:53.250
No, that was scripted.

988
00:48:53.530 --> 00:48:58.310
Well, there are a few deleted scenes that happen right here, Jenna, that are worth mentioning.

989
00:48:58.550 --> 00:49:04.230
One is that there's a few cutaways that they didn't have time for of Meredith trying to leave

990
00:49:04.230 --> 00:49:07.710
the bathroom and then seeing the bat screaming and going back in.

991
00:49:08.030 --> 00:49:09.010
Oh, okay.

992
00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:13.170
You know, so kind of playing up the fact that she really does feel trapped and she really

993
00:49:13.170 --> 00:49:13.690
is scared.

994
00:49:13.690 --> 00:49:18.110
And then there's a scene of Dwight looking through the blinds and screaming at her.

995
00:49:18.310 --> 00:49:21.270
So there's this sort of like buildup of this moment.

996
00:49:21.490 --> 00:49:26.130
And then the other scene that I love that's deleted is as everyone's leaving for the day,

997
00:49:26.450 --> 00:49:30.750
leaving Meredith trapped, Angela and Kelly exit together.

998
00:49:31.070 --> 00:49:34.790
And Angela says, just goes to show you the power of prayer.

999
00:49:35.250 --> 00:49:37.910
And Kelly says, you prayed for this?

1000
00:49:38.090 --> 00:49:40.730
And Angela says, in a general sense.

1001
00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:42.510
Oh, my goodness.

1002
00:49:42.510 --> 00:49:47.350
So did I pray that Meredith needs some kind of reckoning and it's going to happen?

1003
00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:48.190
Wow.

1004
00:49:48.410 --> 00:49:48.850
I know.

1005
00:49:49.130 --> 00:49:54.550
I guess Angela maybe prayed that she would be scared into better behavior.

1006
00:49:55.050 --> 00:49:55.610
Yes.

1007
00:49:55.810 --> 00:49:58.910
And this is, she feels the answer to her prayer.

1008
00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:00.730
Better lifestyle choices, Meredith.

1009
00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:03.970
So now we have the scene.

1010
00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:09.550
The scene of Dwight going in to capture this bat.

1011
00:50:11.090 --> 00:50:14.990
And it ends up landing on Meredith's head.

1012
00:50:15.030 --> 00:50:19.670
So he has to put a trash bag over her head with the bat inside.

1013
00:50:19.670 --> 00:50:22.470
And kind of like wrestle her around.

1014
00:50:23.030 --> 00:50:23.550
Insanity.

1015
00:50:23.790 --> 00:50:26.030
Well, you know, we got a lot of mail about this one, Angela.

1016
00:50:26.450 --> 00:50:33.090
Monica Sweeney, Jessica Bone, Ashley McGettrick, Aaron Westra, and Raphael Bresselsmith.

1017
00:50:33.210 --> 00:50:39.490
And I mean, just countless others said, the scene with Dwight trapping the bat on Meredith's head

1018
00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:41.150
looked so real.

1019
00:50:41.390 --> 00:50:43.510
How did they do it?

1020
00:50:43.870 --> 00:50:46.790
Well, we asked Kate to tell us all about the scene.

1021
00:50:46.790 --> 00:50:51.190
And she told us she worked with both a real bat and a mechanical bat.

1022
00:50:51.590 --> 00:50:52.930
Here's what else she had to say.

1023
00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:55.250
Yes, we had a real bat.

1024
00:51:04.060 --> 00:51:07.400
But we were not allowed to make any noise around the bat.

1025
00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:08.680
You couldn't, I couldn't scream.

1026
00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:12.900
So even though I was screaming, I was acting like I was screaming when I was shooting because

1027
00:51:13.420 --> 00:51:14.400
don't upset the bat.

1028
00:51:14.980 --> 00:51:16.040
Something about the sonar.

1029
00:51:16.220 --> 00:51:21.820
And then we had this motorized thing that I had on my head that we actually did have a stunt double.

1030
00:51:22.220 --> 00:51:25.240
But she, I don't really think they used her.

1031
00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:27.560
And maybe one little shot with her in it.

1032
00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:29.400
One cutaway.

1033
00:51:29.860 --> 00:51:31.680
But I did all of it.

1034
00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:38.600
The motorized thing on the head, it was like sort of like this fluttery thing.

1035
00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:40.140
It was almost like a headband with a motor.

1036
00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:47.120
And then there was a human being with like a puppety thing with a stick.

1037
00:51:47.400 --> 00:51:51.620
He was on his knees and he was trying to put the stick up my skirt before he introduced himself,

1038
00:51:51.740 --> 00:51:53.840
which, whatever, I mean, a little out of order.

1039
00:51:55.320 --> 00:51:57.920
And yes, I could breathe with a bag over my head.

1040
00:51:58.040 --> 00:51:58.720
We figured it out.

1041
00:51:58.860 --> 00:51:59.280
It was fine.

1042
00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:01.220
So I was acting like that.

1043
00:52:01.460 --> 00:52:07.800
And it was one of the most fun scenes I got to do with Rain because he is, he is an amazing

1044
00:52:07.800 --> 00:52:09.680
actor and he goes for it, man.

1045
00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:12.700
So, but I'm not afraid.

1046
00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:16.100
I have never been afraid of Rainn Wilson or Dwight Schrute.

1047
00:52:16.300 --> 00:52:18.340
So that is, that's it.

1048
00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:20.480
Same bad time, same bad channel.

1049
00:52:20.620 --> 00:52:21.600
I just wanted to say that for him.

1050
00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:30.200
Yeah, I guess Phil Shea got this mechanical bat, like a battery-operated Halloween toy, and

1051
00:52:30.200 --> 00:52:37.560
they put it on a headband on her head because it really does look like there are wings flapping

1052
00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:38.660
inside that bag.

1053
00:52:38.780 --> 00:52:43.700
It was, what a good, just old-fashioned movie magic moment.

1054
00:52:43.700 --> 00:52:48.600
Yeah, it was like some new technology and old tech all came together.

1055
00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:50.040
Yes.

1056
00:52:50.140 --> 00:52:56.140
I have a memory, Jenna, of like Kim Ferry and Phil Shea working on that headband.

1057
00:52:56.320 --> 00:52:59.900
Like, I remember it in her hair and makeup area.

1058
00:52:59.900 --> 00:53:00.360
Do you?

1059
00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:03.140
Like, she was like, sort of like, butzing with it and stuff.

1060
00:53:04.020 --> 00:53:06.060
She had it on one of those little mannequins.

1061
00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:06.300
Yeah.

1062
00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:11.200
And he was in there with the little battery-operated controls explaining how it would work and they

1063
00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:13.600
would put a bag over it and they would test it.

1064
00:53:13.860 --> 00:53:15.200
I remember that too.

1065
00:53:15.280 --> 00:53:18.880
We must have been in there in the trailer when they were doing that.

1066
00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:20.020
I have that same memory.

1067
00:53:20.320 --> 00:53:20.500
Oh, man.

1068
00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:24.200
And then on top of it, there was a guy with a stick and a puppet.

1069
00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:24.920
My God.

1070
00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:26.920
What was that about?

1071
00:53:26.920 --> 00:53:27.620
I know, I know.

1072
00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:30.320
I mean, Kate said, like, he had to put a stick up her skirt.

1073
00:53:30.440 --> 00:53:35.280
Like, maybe they had all these different ways the bat might be under the bag.

1074
00:53:36.220 --> 00:53:38.060
Anyway, well done, everyone.

1075
00:53:38.260 --> 00:53:41.260
It was a team effort, but I thought that scene was fantastic.

1076
00:53:42.500 --> 00:53:46.300
Well, the other thing she said in her clip too, Angela, was about how she wasn't allowed

1077
00:53:46.300 --> 00:53:47.440
to actually scream.

1078
00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:48.060
Yes.

1079
00:53:49.300 --> 00:53:53.660
So, when I heard that, I remembered that from our big group scene with the bat.

1080
00:53:53.660 --> 00:53:54.380
Yes, I did too.

1081
00:53:54.380 --> 00:53:57.780
When rain sort of, like, pokes the ceiling up, the real bat is up there.

1082
00:53:57.920 --> 00:53:59.440
That shot was the real bat.

1083
00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:01.360
And we all had to be silent.

1084
00:54:01.660 --> 00:54:06.760
And I remember we had to be super silent as they brought the bat in and placed the bat.

1085
00:54:07.100 --> 00:54:11.700
And then when they removed the bat, before we started doing all of our screaming and reacting

1086
00:54:11.700 --> 00:54:12.260
to nothing.

1087
00:54:12.380 --> 00:54:12.520
Yeah.

1088
00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:13.860
That happens a lot with animals.

1089
00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:18.660
I worked with a squirrel where you had to be super quiet around the squirrel.

1090
00:54:19.040 --> 00:54:19.480
Really?

1091
00:54:19.880 --> 00:54:20.200
Yeah.

1092
00:54:20.460 --> 00:54:22.120
No sudden movements around the squirrel.

1093
00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:28.080
Okay, so now we're back at Pam's art show and Roy has come and he's brought his brother.

1094
00:54:29.220 --> 00:54:29.820
Yes.

1095
00:54:30.540 --> 00:54:39.320
And, um, and, you know, Roy has just regressed, right?

1096
00:54:39.620 --> 00:54:43.660
He says all of this stuff that is so insulting.

1097
00:54:43.660 --> 00:54:47.480
He's like, oh, hey, look at this.

1098
00:54:47.600 --> 00:54:51.640
Like, no one else showed up, but I came and I brought someone.

1099
00:54:52.420 --> 00:54:53.580
That's pretty cool.

1100
00:54:53.860 --> 00:54:58.380
Well, you know, I don't know if it's Roy regressing or is he just trying in his very

1101
00:54:59.500 --> 00:55:07.080
lame attempt here to say, Pam, look, you know, I show up for you, but...

1102
00:55:07.080 --> 00:55:12.620
I know, but like, what if he needs a big congratulations for showing up to his girlfriend's art show?

1103
00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:14.540
Like, you're doing the bare minimum.

1104
00:55:14.920 --> 00:55:20.820
Roy, at his real true self, is going to make the most basic amount of effort and expect that to be

1105
00:55:20.820 --> 00:55:22.160
enough and even applauded.

1106
00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:28.860
Roy is the type of man who needs to be thanked profusely for doing everyday chores, like the

1107
00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:33.600
dishes and taking out the trash, as if he's done something extraordinary, but really, you're just

1108
00:55:33.600 --> 00:55:35.940
contributing to the partnership of running a household.

1109
00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:36.300
Yeah.

1110
00:55:36.460 --> 00:55:37.800
I changed a light bulb.

1111
00:55:37.980 --> 00:55:38.100
Yeah.

1112
00:55:38.180 --> 00:55:38.860
Oh, my gosh.

1113
00:55:38.940 --> 00:55:39.200
Thank you.

1114
00:55:39.520 --> 00:55:45.700
Meanwhile, Roy is not probably thanking Pam for everything she's doing to contribute to running

1115
00:55:45.700 --> 00:55:46.400
the household.

1116
00:55:46.580 --> 00:55:50.620
That's the dynamic that Pam has to look forward to if she follows through on this.

1117
00:55:50.620 --> 00:55:54.560
Well, I definitely think this art show night, she's raw, she's feeling vulnerable,

1118
00:55:54.560 --> 00:56:02.060
and she needs Roy to be more of a support, and he's just not able.

1119
00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:03.720
And I think that's the reality.

1120
00:56:04.100 --> 00:56:09.080
Roy is going to be a great partner to someone else that doesn't need the same things from him

1121
00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:10.560
that Pam needs.

1122
00:56:11.020 --> 00:56:16.000
Well, as he's leaving, he tells her this famous line,

1123
00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:21.020
which is that her art is the prettiest art of all the art.

1124
00:56:21.280 --> 00:56:23.560
Thank you, Roy, for that nugget.

1125
00:56:23.800 --> 00:56:26.880
So I do have a little post-it, and I wrote on it,

1126
00:56:27.420 --> 00:56:29.960
I'm sorry, is this some Gil sass?

1127
00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:31.780
Yes, some Gil sass.

1128
00:56:31.840 --> 00:56:35.360
Yes, because Oscar and Gil show up to the art show.

1129
00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:38.240
They don't know that Pam has walked up behind them.

1130
00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:41.940
And Gil is really kind of going off on your art.

1131
00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:47.580
He's like, listen, real art takes courage and honesty, and this is motel art.

1132
00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:51.520
And Oscar says those aren't Pam's strong points.

1133
00:56:51.700 --> 00:56:55.900
I think all of that hits her so harsh.

1134
00:56:56.120 --> 00:57:01.880
I think it's even more powerful for Pam to hear because in this moment,

1135
00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:08.340
she's forced to hold up a mirror to her life, and part of that courage and honesty

1136
00:57:09.620 --> 00:57:14.020
is finally, boldly saying, Roy is not my person.

1137
00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:22.200
Well, Angela, earlier in this episode, Pam declares that being back with Roy

1138
00:57:22.200 --> 00:57:25.520
shows maturity and dignity, remember?

1139
00:57:25.820 --> 00:57:27.980
And she's like, oh, I hope that didn't sound braggy.

1140
00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:35.280
But when she hears this from Gil and Oscar, she's being confronted with the fact that

1141
00:57:35.280 --> 00:57:42.580
getting back with Roy does not show dignity or honesty or courage.

1142
00:57:43.120 --> 00:57:45.960
Yeah, and I feel like it's one of these beautiful moments on our show where,

1143
00:57:46.620 --> 00:57:50.180
you know, on the one hand, we're talking about her art,

1144
00:57:50.360 --> 00:57:55.740
but on the other hand, for Pam, it's like this self-realization about

1145
00:57:55.740 --> 00:57:57.940
her choices in her life.

1146
00:57:58.380 --> 00:58:02.180
Well, you kind of think that it can't get lower for Pam because

1147
00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:09.400
her only two visitors have been Roy and his brother and Oscar and Gil.

1148
00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:12.800
And then she's standing there alone.

1149
00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:14.500
The evening is winding down.

1150
00:58:15.280 --> 00:58:18.100
She starts to take her art off the wall.

1151
00:58:18.280 --> 00:58:20.200
But then Michael walks in.

1152
00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:21.240
And we hear,

1153
00:58:21.540 --> 00:58:22.940
Pam Casso!

1154
00:58:22.940 --> 00:58:24.080
And you're like, oh, no.

1155
00:58:24.260 --> 00:58:24.640
Oh, no.

1156
00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:25.960
Is this going to be good or bad?

1157
00:58:26.300 --> 00:58:28.380
He's started off with Pam Casso.

1158
00:58:29.620 --> 00:58:31.340
Yeah, it's like, wow.

1159
00:58:31.640 --> 00:58:33.740
And I think that's Pam's reaction.

1160
00:58:34.060 --> 00:58:36.340
Pam's like, oh, Michael, you came.

1161
00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:40.940
And but it's kind of like, I don't even know if I can do this.

1162
00:58:41.180 --> 00:58:48.320
But Michael is immediately just so taken with her art.

1163
00:58:48.320 --> 00:58:51.920
And some of this is because of what he's been through today.

1164
00:58:52.580 --> 00:58:54.940
You know, Michael doesn't know what Pam's been through.

1165
00:58:55.100 --> 00:58:56.920
And Pam doesn't know what Michael's been through.

1166
00:58:57.100 --> 00:59:04.060
But in this moment, these two human beings are coming together and they heal one another.

1167
00:59:04.560 --> 00:59:04.720
Yeah.

1168
00:59:04.980 --> 00:59:05.520
Yeah.

1169
00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:09.040
They're coming together a little hurt and broken from the day.

1170
00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:12.900
And this is such a beautiful scene, Jenna.

1171
00:59:13.160 --> 00:59:14.680
I just love all the beats of it.

1172
00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:16.400
I love how excited Michael is.

1173
00:59:16.580 --> 00:59:19.400
I love how moved Pam is by his excitement.

1174
00:59:19.520 --> 00:59:23.620
And he sees that her car and his car, she painted both of them.

1175
00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:26.040
And it's just such a sweet scene.

1176
00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:28.000
We had a lot of people write in.

1177
00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:36.040
Mary Ruff, Soraya Garlip, Madison Schnurple, Stacey Dahl, and Layla Barocca all asked,

1178
00:59:36.260 --> 00:59:41.840
Jenna, how did you prepare for the level of emotion you had to play in this scene when

1179
00:59:41.840 --> 00:59:44.960
Michael shows up and tells Pam that he's proud of her?

1180
00:59:45.820 --> 00:59:48.340
Did you stay in that emotion during the breaks?

1181
00:59:48.380 --> 00:59:51.540
And if not, was it hard to get back between takes?

1182
00:59:53.080 --> 00:59:54.720
Guys, I will tell you.

1183
00:59:54.720 --> 01:00:01.780
So I didn't have to prepare much for this because my scene partner was Steve Carell.

1184
01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:10.500
And the relationship that I had with Steve in real life, and I think, Angela, this is a

1185
01:00:10.500 --> 01:00:11.640
little bit for you, too.

1186
01:00:13.660 --> 01:00:18.920
He was someone I looked up to in the way that you look up to a mentor or a parent.

1187
01:00:20.700 --> 01:00:23.240
And I admired him so much.

1188
01:00:23.240 --> 01:00:30.200
So just to have him look at me and say, I'm proud of you, I just started crying.

1189
01:00:31.220 --> 01:00:41.020
His sincerity and his truth in saying it, he's a wonderful actor, and it was easy.

1190
01:00:41.340 --> 01:00:41.800
Easy.

1191
01:00:42.080 --> 01:00:43.680
He's just such a special person.

1192
01:00:43.680 --> 01:00:49.700
So to have moments like that with him, the emotion is pretty ready to go.

1193
01:00:49.880 --> 01:00:55.100
I did this appearance on Busy Phillips Talk Show.

1194
01:00:55.260 --> 01:00:56.180
On Busy Tonight.

1195
01:00:56.180 --> 01:00:57.280
I remember.

1196
01:00:58.060 --> 01:00:58.680
Yeah.

1197
01:00:58.880 --> 01:01:01.040
And it was like around my birthday.

1198
01:01:01.480 --> 01:01:07.600
And she had secretly called people from the office to send in gifts for me.

1199
01:01:07.600 --> 01:01:10.360
And you sent in a shirt with a loaf of bread on it.

1200
01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:11.400
Here's the crazy thing.

1201
01:01:11.540 --> 01:01:12.620
She reached out to me.

1202
01:01:12.780 --> 01:01:14.140
She was like, Ange, I want to do this.

1203
01:01:14.580 --> 01:01:15.300
What do you think?

1204
01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:19.980
I said, I am literally online buying her a t-shirt with a loaf of bread on it.

1205
01:01:20.120 --> 01:01:22.860
And she was like, oh my God, can we have that?

1206
01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:26.200
So it was this total surprise.

1207
01:01:26.500 --> 01:01:27.800
And I get all these presents.

1208
01:01:27.800 --> 01:01:31.800
And this whole time that I've been doing this talk show appearance, the entire time,

1209
01:01:31.800 --> 01:01:38.160
there has been a giant box sitting on the set for like 20 minutes, right?

1210
01:01:38.380 --> 01:01:39.020
20 minutes.

1211
01:01:39.340 --> 01:01:39.540
Easily.

1212
01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:43.900
And at the end of the segment, she says, well, we have one last gift for you.

1213
01:01:44.940 --> 01:01:46.600
And it's this giant box.

1214
01:01:46.960 --> 01:01:52.380
And the box pops open and Steve Carell pops out of the box.

1215
01:01:52.560 --> 01:01:57.920
First of all, I was so scared because I wasn't expecting a human being to come out of the box.

1216
01:01:57.920 --> 01:02:04.060
And then as soon as I saw that it was Steve, I just like immediately burst into tears.

1217
01:02:04.320 --> 01:02:10.120
He had sat in a box for like 20 minutes while I droned on giving this interview.

1218
01:02:10.960 --> 01:02:13.200
Just to surprise me for my birthday.

1219
01:02:13.260 --> 01:02:14.400
I don't know.

1220
01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:16.800
It's like, that's Steve, you guys.

1221
01:02:16.980 --> 01:02:21.940
It was such a just generous, like kind, loving thing to do.

1222
01:02:22.160 --> 01:02:25.620
So you just have to, that's how he's always been.

1223
01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:30.400
So in this moment, shooting the scene with him or any emotional scene with him, it's

1224
01:02:30.400 --> 01:02:31.300
all just right there.

1225
01:02:31.460 --> 01:02:33.160
I love that so much, Jenna.

1226
01:02:33.360 --> 01:02:36.540
I hope people can still watch that because that moment is so sweet.

1227
01:02:36.880 --> 01:02:44.440
And you start to cry and Steve is smiling and you guys hug and you say, how long have

1228
01:02:44.440 --> 01:02:45.340
you been in there?

1229
01:02:45.480 --> 01:02:47.120
And he's like, for a while.

1230
01:02:47.320 --> 01:02:50.700
Because they had to set that box before you came out.

1231
01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:51.460
Yes.

1232
01:02:51.460 --> 01:02:55.100
So he was there before the interview and through the whole interview.

1233
01:02:55.460 --> 01:02:55.700
Yes.

1234
01:02:56.040 --> 01:03:01.300
Well, we asked Brent about the art show and how they created this moment between Michael

1235
01:03:01.300 --> 01:03:01.880
and Pam.

1236
01:03:02.320 --> 01:03:10.900
And Jenna, you might want to buckle in because get ready for a love fest from Brent Forrester.

1237
01:03:11.160 --> 01:03:16.060
When people ask me what is my favorite episode of TV that I have ever written, I always say

1238
01:03:16.060 --> 01:03:16.820
business school.

1239
01:03:16.820 --> 01:03:20.360
And partly it's because the episode is so nicely designed.

1240
01:03:20.360 --> 01:03:24.920
It's got that big comedy sea story with the bat and the vampire.

1241
01:03:25.290 --> 01:03:30.080
It's got a big comedy performance story with Michael going to business school.

1242
01:03:30.260 --> 01:03:34.720
But above all, it has this tremendous emotional story with Pam's art show.

1243
01:03:35.120 --> 01:03:40.160
I think that what is happening in Pam's art show is one of those wonderful coincidences

1244
01:03:40.160 --> 01:03:45.600
of the experience of the writer, me, and the actress, Jenna, coming together.

1245
01:03:45.600 --> 01:03:52.160
If you try to be an artist of any kind, a writer, an actress, any artist at all, you have to

1246
01:03:52.160 --> 01:03:54.280
believe in yourself so profoundly.

1247
01:03:54.780 --> 01:04:01.100
And inevitably, there will come moments of failure where your belief in yourself is dashed.

1248
01:04:01.200 --> 01:04:04.720
And it is so painful and unforgettable when it happens to a human being.

1249
01:04:04.880 --> 01:04:07.920
And I think it's really what Jenna is expressing at the end of this episode.

1250
01:04:07.920 --> 01:04:12.840
If you read her tremendous book, An Actor's Life, which is both an instructional manual and

1251
01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:19.600
a riveting autobiography, you will learn that Jenna Fisher spent seven years trying to break

1252
01:04:19.600 --> 01:04:21.180
into the business of acting.

1253
01:04:21.420 --> 01:04:24.200
At the end of seven years, she felt that she had failed.

1254
01:04:24.200 --> 01:04:28.160
She packed up her car and she decided she was going to return home to Missouri.

1255
01:04:28.700 --> 01:04:32.540
She was only persuaded to stay by her acting coach and her manager who said,

1256
01:04:32.780 --> 01:04:35.400
please, Jenna, spend one more season trying out for TV.

1257
01:04:35.400 --> 01:04:36.820
And that's when she got the office.

1258
01:04:36.940 --> 01:04:41.220
But that experience of terrible defeat is something that I believe she never forgot.

1259
01:04:41.220 --> 01:04:43.740
And it's what she's expressing at the end of this episode.

1260
01:04:44.460 --> 01:04:49.700
Steve Carell says that when he watches Business School, that moment where Michael Scott hugs

1261
01:04:49.700 --> 01:04:55.980
Pam and there's a close on her face, Steve says he can see five different emotions play

1262
01:04:55.980 --> 01:04:58.180
through Jenna's face in that performance.

1263
01:04:58.180 --> 01:05:01.180
It is heartbreak and it is gratitude.

1264
01:05:01.180 --> 01:05:06.420
It is just the whole human experience wrapped up in a single take.

1265
01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:08.440
It's a tremendous performance.

1266
01:05:08.820 --> 01:05:14.760
And it's part of why I will always be so grateful that I got to write this episode for one of my

1267
01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:17.080
favorite actresses of all time, Jenna Fisher.

1268
01:05:17.560 --> 01:05:18.840
Oh my gosh, Brent.

1269
01:05:19.400 --> 01:05:21.480
What do you say after that?

1270
01:05:21.660 --> 01:05:24.280
That is the most beautiful thing ever.

1271
01:05:24.280 --> 01:05:27.780
Like Jenna, that is so lovely.

1272
01:05:28.740 --> 01:05:29.760
You know what?

1273
01:05:29.900 --> 01:05:37.420
I am going to save that and I'm going to listen to that anytime I am having a bad day because

1274
01:05:38.220 --> 01:05:40.180
that really, thank you.

1275
01:05:40.320 --> 01:05:41.240
That lifts me up.

1276
01:05:41.520 --> 01:05:43.860
Jenna, I have been at all of your big life moments.

1277
01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:44.820
Really, I have.

1278
01:05:44.940 --> 01:05:47.840
I have heard toast and speeches about you.

1279
01:05:48.100 --> 01:05:50.220
And that's probably the beSt.

1280
01:05:51.560 --> 01:05:53.000
I was at your wedding.

1281
01:05:53.460 --> 01:05:55.320
Brent should have given a toast at your wedding.

1282
01:05:56.000 --> 01:05:56.400
Clearly.

1283
01:05:56.860 --> 01:05:57.240
Clearly.

1284
01:05:57.600 --> 01:05:58.460
Oh my gosh.

1285
01:05:58.580 --> 01:06:01.820
He like recapped your book and your whole life experience.

1286
01:06:02.020 --> 01:06:05.160
And then what Steve said was so beautiful.

1287
01:06:05.820 --> 01:06:08.080
I mean, Jenna, Brent was going on for so long.

1288
01:06:08.280 --> 01:06:09.160
He ran out of air.

1289
01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:10.180
Did you hear at the end?

1290
01:06:10.280 --> 01:06:10.520
He had to go.

1291
01:06:11.700 --> 01:06:14.300
He was so passionate talking about you.

1292
01:06:14.660 --> 01:06:15.700
Everything he said is true.

1293
01:06:15.840 --> 01:06:16.320
There's one thing.

1294
01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:19.360
I never actually packed up my car and said I was going to leave.

1295
01:06:19.360 --> 01:06:24.440
But I did say I called my managers after seven years of failures and said, I've decided

1296
01:06:24.440 --> 01:06:25.840
to become a vet technician.

1297
01:06:26.460 --> 01:06:27.900
I had signed up.

1298
01:06:28.000 --> 01:06:29.440
It was a two-year program.

1299
01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:33.740
I'd been doing some animal rescue and I was going to do it full time.

1300
01:06:33.740 --> 01:06:37.100
And I wanted my vet tech license so that I could administer medicines.

1301
01:06:37.120 --> 01:06:39.580
And so I was like, I'm out of here.

1302
01:06:40.120 --> 01:06:46.640
And it is true that my manager and my acting coach sort of yelled at me.

1303
01:06:46.640 --> 01:06:49.240
And they were like, what did you think an acting career was?

1304
01:06:49.400 --> 01:06:50.940
You thought it was just a bunch of ups?

1305
01:06:51.120 --> 01:06:53.800
Just a bunch of successes?

1306
01:06:54.240 --> 01:06:54.560
No.

1307
01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:57.860
It's little successes followed by many failures.

1308
01:06:58.220 --> 01:07:00.060
This is what being an actor is.

1309
01:07:00.160 --> 01:07:06.280
And it's true what Brent says too, which is that I did channel a lot of those struggling

1310
01:07:06.280 --> 01:07:08.180
years into my performance for Pam.

1311
01:07:08.180 --> 01:07:14.160
I literally sat at a reception desk and dreamed of being an artiSt.

1312
01:07:14.460 --> 01:07:16.060
I have done that.

1313
01:07:16.220 --> 01:07:17.580
I did it for seven years.

1314
01:07:17.880 --> 01:07:21.940
So I deeply, deeply felt Pam's longing.

1315
01:07:23.140 --> 01:07:29.180
Brent also sent us in an audio clip where he discussed just the overall story for this

1316
01:07:29.180 --> 01:07:34.040
episode with Pam and Michael and why he thinks that it resonates so much with people.

1317
01:07:34.320 --> 01:07:37.260
And here's what he had to say about that.

1318
01:07:37.260 --> 01:07:42.740
I remember before I was sent off to write this episode, I got into a big debate with

1319
01:07:42.740 --> 01:07:46.560
Greg Daniels, the show creator, about the story of Pam's art show.

1320
01:07:46.740 --> 01:07:49.800
And I, at the time, thought of myself as a sophisticated storyteller.

1321
01:07:49.920 --> 01:07:55.320
And I took the position that this story did not work because, I said to Greg, a story is

1322
01:07:55.320 --> 01:07:59.620
about a protagonist pursuing a goal, overcoming obstacles, and in the end, accomplishing that

1323
01:07:59.620 --> 01:07:59.840
goal.

1324
01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:03.480
And in this case, Pam's goal is to be respected as an artiSt.

1325
01:08:03.480 --> 01:08:08.080
But when Michael Scott buys her painting at the end, it doesn't resolve her problem because

1326
01:08:08.080 --> 01:08:10.960
she does not respect Michael Scott's taste.

1327
01:08:11.300 --> 01:08:15.020
If so, facto, her problem is not resolved and the story does not work.

1328
01:08:15.280 --> 01:08:20.100
And I remember Greg saying to me, Brent, this is a sophisticated story with a sophisticated

1329
01:08:20.100 --> 01:08:20.600
ending.

1330
01:08:20.800 --> 01:08:21.940
It would be like this.

1331
01:08:21.940 --> 01:08:27.260
Suppose you, Brent, were the protagonist and your goal was to impress me with your storytelling

1332
01:08:27.260 --> 01:08:27.740
abilities.

1333
01:08:27.780 --> 01:08:32.000
But you said something so stupid in the breaking of this story that I fired you and sent you

1334
01:08:32.000 --> 01:08:32.280
home.

1335
01:08:32.460 --> 01:08:35.200
You went home miserable and not having accomplished your goal.

1336
01:08:35.360 --> 01:08:39.880
But your family dog came up to you, cuddled with you on the couch, and put its head on

1337
01:08:39.880 --> 01:08:40.320
your lap.

1338
01:08:40.560 --> 01:08:47.440
This would be an emotional resolution and consolation, even though your stated goal was not achieved.

1339
01:08:47.440 --> 01:08:52.300
Greg was so right about this, and it was so funny the way he put it.

1340
01:08:52.760 --> 01:08:58.240
You know, Greg, his body of work is characterized by these sophisticated endings where the character

1341
01:08:58.240 --> 01:09:03.720
does not get what they think they want, but still gets what they emotionally need at the

1342
01:09:03.720 --> 01:09:03.980
end.

1343
01:09:04.080 --> 01:09:08.180
And it's part of the reason why I'm so grateful to have worked on this show and worked with

1344
01:09:08.180 --> 01:09:12.600
Greg Daniels, who taught me so many priceless lessons about storytelling.

1345
01:09:13.300 --> 01:09:16.060
Oh, Jenna, we both feel like we know Greg.

1346
01:09:16.060 --> 01:09:17.900
Greg, we've known him a long time.

1347
01:09:18.320 --> 01:09:23.520
But when I hear these stories from the writer's room, it's this whole new perspective on Greg.

1348
01:09:23.800 --> 01:09:29.120
And man, I am just more and more impressed with him and how he can convey a thought or

1349
01:09:29.120 --> 01:09:32.400
explain the dynamics between human relationships.

1350
01:09:32.460 --> 01:09:35.060
And he's so flippin' smart.

1351
01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:36.140
It's so true.

1352
01:09:36.300 --> 01:09:39.620
And it is such a sophisticated way to tell a story.

1353
01:09:40.520 --> 01:09:42.560
I mean, they've written songs about that idea.

1354
01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:45.060
You don't get what you want, but you get what you need.

1355
01:09:45.060 --> 01:09:45.620
Yeah.

1356
01:09:45.860 --> 01:09:48.500
And that is what happens in this story.

1357
01:09:48.860 --> 01:09:49.020
Yeah.

1358
01:09:49.320 --> 01:09:50.560
It's so layered.

1359
01:09:50.800 --> 01:09:54.660
Pam and Michael heal one another and give one another what they need.

1360
01:09:56.160 --> 01:09:56.640
Yeah.

1361
01:09:56.820 --> 01:09:56.920
Yeah.

1362
01:09:57.100 --> 01:09:58.600
Someone to say, I'm proud of you.

1363
01:09:58.800 --> 01:10:04.080
And someone to show Michael, I guess, what the power of a piece of paper is.

1364
01:10:04.440 --> 01:10:05.480
That's what he says.

1365
01:10:05.640 --> 01:10:06.300
It's so sweet.

1366
01:10:06.560 --> 01:10:08.380
Well, and also just to be appreciated.

1367
01:10:08.800 --> 01:10:10.740
Pam really appreciated that he came there.

1368
01:10:10.740 --> 01:10:18.040
And it's a reminder for Michael that, you know, business is people and you can't cancel people.

1369
01:10:19.020 --> 01:10:19.980
That's right.

1370
01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:25.620
We had a question from Amber, Julie, Aubrey, and Rohit.

1371
01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:29.560
Did anyone end up keeping the picture of the office that Pam made?

1372
01:10:29.560 --> 01:10:30.880
And, yep, me.

1373
01:10:31.220 --> 01:10:34.160
We talked about that in Casino Night with John.

1374
01:10:34.320 --> 01:10:34.720
Yes.

1375
01:10:35.500 --> 01:10:40.800
That I got to keep that and I have it hanging down in my entryway of my house.

1376
01:10:41.460 --> 01:10:44.660
And a little thank you to Phil Shea for making that happen for Jenna.

1377
01:10:44.900 --> 01:10:52.780
Well, we wrap up this episode finding out that Ryan has not lost his job, that Michael has just

1378
01:10:52.780 --> 01:10:58.360
moved him to the annex, which is such a punishment because now he's going to have to share a desk

1379
01:10:58.360 --> 01:11:03.180
pod with Kelly and Kelly is so excited.

1380
01:11:03.380 --> 01:11:04.600
She's so thrilled.

1381
01:11:04.780 --> 01:11:07.500
And here's a little tidbit from the DVD commentary.

1382
01:11:08.000 --> 01:11:13.120
Brent said he went into Mindy's office and he said, Mindy, here's the scene.

1383
01:11:13.280 --> 01:11:14.520
And he pitched the scene to her.

1384
01:11:15.000 --> 01:11:19.600
And she began to just improvise as Kelly, how Kelly would respond to this news.

1385
01:11:19.780 --> 01:11:22.220
And Brent just wrote it all down verbatim.

1386
01:11:22.760 --> 01:11:29.140
So he was like, he said, Mindy is so great at channeling Kelly that you literally could go in

1387
01:11:29.140 --> 01:11:30.740
and pitch to her a Kelly moment.

1388
01:11:30.900 --> 01:11:32.640
And she'd be like, oh, she would totally say this.

1389
01:11:33.840 --> 01:11:35.140
I love that.

1390
01:11:35.140 --> 01:11:38.180
Well, we had some fan questions about this.

1391
01:11:38.660 --> 01:11:45.640
Haley, Fortino, Jillian, Audrey, and Marielle all asked, was Michael moving Ryan to the annex

1392
01:11:45.640 --> 01:11:52.640
as a punishment, a way to get Ryan back out of the bullpen so that he wasn't as visible and

1393
01:11:52.640 --> 01:11:54.400
could be in the writer's room some more?

1394
01:11:54.520 --> 01:11:55.240
I'm sure.

1395
01:11:55.880 --> 01:11:56.520
Yes.

1396
01:11:56.620 --> 01:11:56.840
Yeah.

1397
01:11:57.040 --> 01:12:00.480
Because now our three writers were all back there.

1398
01:12:00.680 --> 01:12:02.620
Paul, BJ, and Mindy.

1399
01:12:02.780 --> 01:12:03.860
No, it was very smart.

1400
01:12:05.100 --> 01:12:10.620
Well, Jenna, there are some deleted scenes, you know, in this episode that are really fantastic.

1401
01:12:10.620 --> 01:12:14.740
I haven't talked too much about them, but there are two that are sort of interesting.

1402
01:12:14.740 --> 01:12:17.220
And I thought I'd share a little bit with you about that.

1403
01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:19.260
And we had some fan questions.

1404
01:12:19.580 --> 01:12:20.780
So here's the first one.

1405
01:12:20.780 --> 01:12:27.020
And Ryan Moberly, Jamie Kearns, Allie Giancarlo, and Jamie Miller all wrote in and they said,

1406
01:12:27.220 --> 01:12:33.780
in the deleted scenes, Karen attempts to rip down Pam's art show flyer in the kitchen.

1407
01:12:34.860 --> 01:12:35.620
All right.

1408
01:12:35.820 --> 01:12:39.800
They say, I know there are so many people that say that Karen didn't deserve all of this hate

1409
01:12:39.800 --> 01:12:41.780
because her only crime was not being Pam.

1410
01:12:41.780 --> 01:12:43.820
But can we please discuss?

1411
01:12:45.560 --> 01:12:46.040
Yes.

1412
01:12:47.020 --> 01:12:48.600
So what happens, Angela?

1413
01:12:48.860 --> 01:12:49.000
Okay.

1414
01:12:49.220 --> 01:12:50.500
Well, Karen is in the kitchen.

1415
01:12:50.660 --> 01:12:52.080
There's a flyer for Pam's art show.

1416
01:12:52.240 --> 01:12:56.640
And she looks around and she reaches up to grab it to pull it off the wall.

1417
01:12:57.040 --> 01:13:00.540
And then I think Oscar walks in and she just quickly just leaves.

1418
01:13:02.200 --> 01:13:04.760
And we don't really know anything more than that.

1419
01:13:04.920 --> 01:13:05.840
Just little glimpse.

1420
01:13:06.600 --> 01:13:10.020
But, you know, everyone, everyone in the office knew about it.

1421
01:13:10.220 --> 01:13:12.740
I don't know that she was being that vindictive.

1422
01:13:12.860 --> 01:13:14.440
Maybe she just wanted to see the details.

1423
01:13:14.620 --> 01:13:18.260
But you could say maybe she didn't want Jim to see it.

1424
01:13:19.540 --> 01:13:21.220
Maybe she didn't want Jim to want to go.

1425
01:13:23.040 --> 01:13:25.080
Maybe she wasn't being vindictive.

1426
01:13:25.380 --> 01:13:27.820
I mean, she was going to take it off the wall.

1427
01:13:27.820 --> 01:13:33.420
Oh, I think this was a good cut because I don't like this side of Karen.

1428
01:13:33.700 --> 01:13:36.260
I'm glad that this was cut from her story.

1429
01:13:36.440 --> 01:13:39.220
Me too, because Karen was already so vilified.

1430
01:13:39.380 --> 01:13:41.520
She didn't need to lean into it, you know?

1431
01:13:41.680 --> 01:13:42.300
Yeah, I agree.

1432
01:13:42.300 --> 01:13:44.640
So I think that was a good scene to have deleted.

1433
01:13:44.900 --> 01:13:48.140
Now, here's another deleted scene that Kate Flannery and I talked about.

1434
01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:51.440
And Susan Lewis and Kyle Meeker wrote in and said,

1435
01:13:51.580 --> 01:13:55.520
there's a deleted scene where Meredith is telling Oscar a joke about Pam and Roy.

1436
01:13:55.520 --> 01:13:58.840
The joke is almost all bleeped out.

1437
01:13:59.080 --> 01:14:00.480
What was the joke?

1438
01:14:01.680 --> 01:14:02.200
Oh, no.

1439
01:14:02.400 --> 01:14:04.760
This is like us doing laundry and betrayal.

1440
01:14:05.120 --> 01:14:05.170
I know.

1441
01:14:05.280 --> 01:14:05.520
I know.

1442
01:14:05.760 --> 01:14:07.860
So I said, Kate, what was that all about?

1443
01:14:08.020 --> 01:14:09.360
I went and watched the scene.

1444
01:14:09.700 --> 01:14:13.480
And the deleted scene is Angela's in the kitchen and Meredith's in there.

1445
01:14:13.560 --> 01:14:17.040
And she's like, hey, why did Pam leave the wedding with Roy?

1446
01:14:17.800 --> 01:14:19.900
And I'm just like, would you stop it?

1447
01:14:20.000 --> 01:14:21.080
And I leave the kitchen.

1448
01:14:21.280 --> 01:14:22.860
I am not going to entertain this.

1449
01:14:23.040 --> 01:14:24.680
Then Oscar comes out of the bathroom.

1450
01:14:24.680 --> 01:14:29.620
Meredith goes, hey, Oscar, why did Pam leave the wedding with Roy?

1451
01:14:29.860 --> 01:14:32.100
And Oscar's like, Meredith, I'm not going to get into this.

1452
01:14:32.200 --> 01:14:34.320
I think gossip is like harmful.

1453
01:14:34.780 --> 01:14:36.600
And then Meredith can't even contain herself.

1454
01:14:36.780 --> 01:14:38.660
And she starts to tell the rest of her joke.

1455
01:14:38.800 --> 01:14:43.420
And she kind of does a gesture with her hands by her crotch area.

1456
01:14:43.620 --> 01:14:47.400
And it just is like, because Roy bleep bleep bleep bleep bleep bleep bleep.

1457
01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:50.080
And then Oscar's like, oh, gross.

1458
01:14:50.320 --> 01:14:54.660
And Meredith cackles and says, I know, and leaves the kitchen.

1459
01:14:56.380 --> 01:15:06.280
And Kate said, Kate said that in the script, all it said was, Meredith tells an off-color joke to Oscar.

1460
01:15:06.940 --> 01:15:07.720
That's it.

1461
01:15:07.800 --> 01:15:09.280
And it didn't say anything else.

1462
01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:10.060
Is that true, Jenna?

1463
01:15:10.060 --> 01:15:12.320
Yes, I checked the script.

1464
01:15:12.560 --> 01:15:16.700
And it says, Meredith has one scripted line.

1465
01:15:17.060 --> 01:15:19.760
Then the script says, Meredith says, Roy.

1466
01:15:20.600 --> 01:15:30.080
Then long bleeped sentence while gesturing in an unusual way, ambiguously, that could be totally nasty or could be something quite different.

1467
01:15:30.340 --> 01:15:31.220
Hard to tell, really.

1468
01:15:31.480 --> 01:15:33.240
There's no visible lip flap.

1469
01:15:34.040 --> 01:15:37.560
And then the script says, Oscar says, that's disgusting.

1470
01:15:37.920 --> 01:15:40.060
So I said, Kate, do you remember what you said?

1471
